  "hints": [
    "write out fib(5) as a recursion tree - which subproblems get computed more than once?",
    "what if you built the sequence forward from the base cases instead of recursing down from n?",
    "at each step, the next term only depends on the two terms immediately before it",
    "iterate from 0 up to n, keeping just prev and prev2, updating them each step"
  ],
  "commonMistakes": [
    "mixing up the base cases (treating fib(1) as 0 or fib(0) as 1)",
    "using unmemoized recursion and being surprised it's slow for n around 30",
    "off-by-one when translating a 1-indexed mental model into this 0-indexed definition"
  ],
  "content": {
    "problemStatement": "The Fibonacci sequence starts with 0 and 1, and every term after that is\nthe sum of the two terms before it: 0, 1, 1, 2, 3, 5, 8, 13, ...\n\n**Given an integer `n`, return the n-th term of this sequence.**\n\nThe sequence is 0-indexed, so the 0th term is 0 and the 1st term is 1.\n",
    "constraints": [
      "0 <= n <= 30",
      "term 0 is 0 and term 1 is 1"
    ],
    "notes": [
      "n = 0 (base case, returns 0)",
      "n = 1 (base case, returns 1)",
      "larger n where naive recursion becomes very slow"
    ],
    "inputFormat": "Arguments passed into solution function",
    "outputFormat": "Return output matching method signature",
